Source of motanka-stack-shell: Quickshell 0.3.2, the desktop shell of
upstream Omarchy v4.5.0, and cpptrace 1.0.4 (shared, with libunwind and a
bundled libdwarf-lite); omasnap 1.26.2, Upstream's screenshot tool, and
layer-shell-qt 6.8.3, the Qt library omasnap draws its capture overlay
with; ttfx 0.5.1, the text effects program of Upstream's screensaver. All
under /opt/motanka, for both suites. On resolute only it also has
hyprland-preview-share-picker 0.3.0.r3.g07602ef, the screen share picker
of Upstream's xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland configuration.

The upstream tarballs are listed in stack/shell-components.tsv (imported by
build-imports); test/vm/build fetches them with stack/fetch-sources
--components stack/shell-components.tsv.

Both suites build against motanka-qt6-dev (Qt 6.12 in /opt/motanka) and
the package depends on motanka-qt6: Upstream's shell needs Qt 6.11 or
later, and both archives carry 6.10 (design.md, "Qt for both releases").
motanka-qt6 and motanka-qt6-dev are locally built .debs, given to sbuild
with --extra-package (sbuild-local), so packaging/motanka-qt6 must be built
for the suite first. The build stops if the archive's Qt development files
are in the chroot, and every Qt library that quickshell, omasnap,
libLayerShellQtInterface and layer-shell-qt's plugins load must resolve
from /opt/motanka/lib.
  noble     also gets motanka-stack-libs (GCC 16's C++ runtime, which
            motanka-qt6 depends on there), so packaging/motanka-stack is
            built first too. GCC 16 comes from the toolchain PPA
            (sbuild-repos). extra-cmake-modules needs CMake 3.30 and noble
            has 3.28, so the build first compiles the Stack's CMake 4.5.0
            into debian/shell-build/tools and configures
            extra-cmake-modules, layer-shell-qt and omasnap with it.
  resolute  the archive's GCC 16, C++ runtime and CMake.

Quickshell and layer-shell-qt use Qt's private API, so the package depends
on exactly the motanka-qt6 version it was built against
(debian/shell-build.sh shlibs). The archive's layer-shell-qt cannot be used
for the same reason: it is built against the archive's Qt.

omasnap:
  - debian/patches/omasnap-wayland-protocols-dir.patch makes the folder of
    the wayland-protocols XML files a cache variable; the build points it
    at the staged wayland-protocols 1.50 on both suites (24.04's 1.34 has
    no ext-image-capture-source or ext-image-copy-capture).
  - Its smoke suite (the omasnap_tests binary) runs during the build with
    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen and a private XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, as Upstream's
    PKGBUILD does; DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck skips it. The runtime and
    temporary folders are short ones under /tmp: omasnap's Unix sockets
    live below them, and a socket path longer than 108 bytes is silently
    skipped. Its OCR check reads text back with tesseract, so tesseract-ocr,
    tesseract-ocr-eng, fontconfig and a font (fonts-dejavu-core) are
    <!nocheck> build dependencies. A failure is run once more (the suite
    waits at most 1.5 s or 5 s in places); a second failure fails the build
    and prints the check's exit code. Its clang-tidy test is not run: the
    chroot has no run-clang-tidy.
  - Installed files: /opt/motanka/bin/omasnap, its menu entry
    /opt/motanka/share/applications/omasnap.desktop and icon
    /opt/motanka/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/omasnap.svg. Only the
    Motanka session has /opt/motanka/share in XDG_DATA_DIRS, so other
    sessions never list the entry. The licences of the fonts and icons
    built into the binary are in /usr/share/doc/motanka-stack-shell/omasnap.
  - At run time it calls hyprctl (motanka-stack-hyprland), wl-copy and
    wl-paste, tesseract, xdg-open and omarchy-notification-send; the
    package depends on tesseract-ocr, tesseract-ocr-eng, wl-clipboard and
    xdg-utils, as Upstream's package does. It captures the screen through
    the compositor's ext-image-copy-capture-v1.

ttfx:
  - Upstream's Rust port of terminaltexteffects (omacom/ttfx).
    bin/omarchy-screensaver runs it in a loop and polls for it with pgrep,
    so without it the idle screensaver spins and cannot be dismissed.
  - Its component has build "cargo". stack/fetch-sources reads the
    Cargo.lock inside the ttfx tarball, refuses any package not from the
    crates.io registry, downloads every crate it lists, checks each against
    the lock file's SHA-256 and packs them reproducibly into one more orig
    component, motanka-stack-shell_<V>.orig-ttfx-crates.tar.gz (folder
    ttfx-crates/<crate>-<version>/ with a .cargo-checksum.json each).
    packaging/build.sh refuses to build without it.
  - The build runs cargo-1.91 build --release --frozen --offline with
    rustc-1.91 (both releases carry Rust 1.91 in the archive; noble's
    unversioned cargo is too old for some crates). CARGO_HOME has a
    config.toml that replaces crates.io with the directory source
    ttfx-crates/; the network-less chroot is never asked for anything.
  - Installed file: /opt/motanka/bin/ttfx. It loads only libc and libgcc_s
    from the host, so it has no RUNPATH; rustc's defaults give it PIE and
    full RELRO. The test step runs ttfx --version from the staging tree.

hyprland-preview-share-picker (resolute only):
  - Upstream's config/hypr/xdph.conf sets screencopy:custom_picker_binary
    to hyprland-preview-share-picker, and xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland 1.5.0
    fails the share request ("Could not open the share picker.") when that
    program cannot start; there is no fallback. The Stack builds xdph for
    26.04 only and 24.04 has no gtk4-layer-shell, so noble gets neither,
    and packages.yaml skips the picker there.
  - Pinned to the commit of Upstream's PKGBUILD (omacom/omarchy-pkgs):
    v0.3.0 plus WhySoBad/hyprland-preview-share-picker#30 (shm pool sizing).
    Build "cargo", like ttfx, with the crates component
    hyprland-preview-share-picker-crates; the components file lists it for
    resolute, so noble's build unpacks it but skips it.
  - debian/patches/hyprland-preview-share-picker-build-rs.patch replaces
    build.rs with the PKGBUILD's, which sets GIT_VERSION to
    v0.3.0-r3-g07602ef; the original asks git and panics without a
    repository.
  - lib/hyprland-protocols is an empty git-submodule folder in the tarball.
    The build replaces it with a symbolic link to the hyprland-protocols
    component (the Stack's 0.8.0 tarball, build "source", resolute only);
    the one file it reads, protocols/hyprland-toplevel-export-v1.xml, is
    byte-identical to the submodule commit the PKGBUILD uses. debian/rules
    clean puts the empty folder back.
  - Build-Depends on resolute: libgtk-4-dev and libgtk4-layer-shell-dev
    (#if resolute in debian/control.in). The run-time libraries (GTK 4,
    gtk4-layer-shell, GLib, cairo, pango, ...) come from ${shlibs:Depends}.
  - Installed files: /opt/motanka/bin/hyprland-preview-share-picker (no
    RUNPATH, host libraries only) and
    /opt/motanka/share/hyprland-preview-share-picker/schema.json, written by
    the built program's schema subcommand during the build. The test step
    checks that --version prints v0.3.0-r3-g07602ef.
  - It runs only through xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland, which finds it on the
    PATH of the systemd user manager; the Motanka session puts
    /opt/motanka/bin there. xdph and hyprland are in motanka-stack-hyprland,
    which motanka-stack pulls in; this package does not depend on it.
    Upstream's optional dependency slurp (region selection) is not a
    dependency either.

layer-shell-qt ships libLayerShellQtInterface in /opt/motanka/lib, the Qt
Wayland shell integration plugin in
/opt/motanka/lib/qt6/plugins/wayland-shell-integration and the QML module
org.kde.layershell in /opt/motanka/lib/qt6/qml, where motanka-qt6's Qt
looks. Headers, CMake files and tests are not shipped.
